1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,700
AZ Alkmaar — Football Intelligence Platform

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,706
Clubs generate huge amounts of match video,

3
00:00:06,706 --> 00:00:10,959
but turning that footage into reliable, structured information is still hard.

4
00:00:10,959 --> 00:00:14,438
This is a football intelligence platform for AZ Alkmaar:

5
00:00:14,438 --> 00:00:19,077
a pipeline that takes raw match video and turns it into validated,

6
00:00:19,077 --> 00:00:23,330
structured football data, and eventually, football intelligence you can act on.

7
00:00:23,330 --> 00:00:29,635
We don't ask an AI model to watch an entire ninety minute match and guess what happened.

8
00:00:29,635 --> 00:00:32,232
Instead, the platform divides video into controlled,

9
00:00:32,232 --> 00:00:34,086
thirty to sixty second segments.

10
00:00:34,086 --> 00:00:38,166
Each segment is small enough to trace, test, and validate independently,

11
00:00:38,166 --> 00:00:43,730
the discipline that turns a demo trick into an engineering pipeline you can actually trust.

12
00:00:43,730 --> 00:00:48,204
Here is the segment builder, running against real Alfheim match footage.

13
00:00:48,204 --> 00:00:51,458
An operator selects a starting point and duration,

14
00:00:51,458 --> 00:00:55,118
and the platform prepares a playable window, this one,

15
00:00:55,118 --> 00:00:57,965
fifteen to sixteen minutes into the match.

16
00:00:57,965 --> 00:01:01,219
Behind the scenes it extracts frames, caches detections,

17
00:01:01,219 --> 00:01:05,693
and prepares everything the analysis pipeline needs. Segments can be grouped,

18
00:01:05,693 --> 00:01:07,319
replayed, and reprocessed independently,

19
00:01:07,319 --> 00:01:10,573
so every result traces back to an exact,

20
00:01:10,573 --> 00:01:12,200
reproducible slice of video.

21
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:15,925
Inside each segment, computer vision detects players, the ball,

22
00:01:15,925 --> 00:01:20,478
and team shape, and proposes candidate football events, passes, turnovers, shots,

23
00:01:20,478 --> 00:01:25,031
fouls, each with a timestamp, a team, and a confidence score.

24
00:01:25,031 --> 00:01:29,583
But a raw AI prediction is a proposal, not a fact.

25
00:01:29,583 --> 00:01:36,619
Every candidate still has to earn its place in the statistics by passing through football rules validation.

26
00:01:36,619 --> 00:01:41,172
That principle sits underneath everything else you'll see: predictions get questioned,

27
00:01:41,172 --> 00:01:41,1000
never assumed.

28
00:01:41,1000 --> 00:01:44,532
This is the Global Football Rules Reviewer.

29
00:01:44,532 --> 00:01:51,406
Every candidate event is checked against the actual I-FAB Laws of the Game and the project's own analytics definitions,

30
00:01:51,406 --> 00:01:56,109
then independently reconstructed from the video before it's compared to the engine's output.

31
00:01:56,109 --> 00:02:00,088
In this fifteen to sixteen minute segment, fifteen proposals were reviewed.

32
00:02:00,088 --> 00:02:02,982
Fourteen ended up matching confirmed engine events exactly,

33
00:02:02,982 --> 00:02:04,429
most lined up directly,

34
00:02:04,429 --> 00:02:09,494
and one engine event was independently verified after its proposal didn't correspond to it.

35
00:02:09,494 --> 00:02:15,644
The one remaining proposal that didn't match a distinct engine event was excluded from the published statistics,

36
00:02:15,644 --> 00:02:19,262
and match state annotations stayed out of the numbers too.

37
00:02:19,262 --> 00:02:25,050
All one hundred and five protected regression tests passed before this segment was published and locked.

38
00:02:25,050 --> 00:02:29,941
Only validated events feed the statistics layer. Possession, passes, pass completion,

39
00:02:29,941 --> 00:02:33,944
fouls, and interceptions are implemented and undergoing thorough validation.

40
00:02:33,944 --> 00:02:37,946
Shots and shots on target are currently in review.

41
00:02:37,946 --> 00:02:42,837
Corners are near completion. Offsides, attacking and defensive actions, and player,

42
00:02:42,837 --> 00:02:46,395
team, and formation statistics are still in development.

43
00:02:46,395 --> 00:02:52,620
We would rather show you exactly where each statistic stands than call everything finished.

44
00:02:52,620 --> 00:02:56,391
Once data is validated, Copilot can reason over it directly,

45
00:02:56,391 --> 00:02:59,784
how many passes were completed, how sure are we,

46
00:02:59,784 --> 00:03:01,292
what still needs review.

47
00:03:01,292 --> 00:03:05,817
Because it is answering from validated structured events rather than raw predictions,

48
00:03:05,817 --> 00:03:09,965
the insight is only as reliable as the validation behind it.

49
00:03:09,965 --> 00:03:11,850
That is exactly the point.

50
00:03:11,850 --> 00:03:15,021
The same architecture can extend to tactical patterns, scouting,

51
00:03:15,021 --> 00:03:18,898
and player development, including post processed views like player heat maps,

52
00:03:18,898 --> 00:03:22,421
pitch zone identification, and shot placement and attribution by player,

53
00:03:22,421 --> 00:03:26,650
built from the full recorded clip rather than the live statistics screen.

54
00:03:26,650 --> 00:03:29,116
It can also run in a private,

55
00:03:29,116 --> 00:03:31,231
on premises environment where that's required,

56
00:03:31,231 --> 00:03:40,040
including a future concept where a main camera queries neighbouring goal end and touchline cameras for a second opinion when its own confidence is low,

57
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:42,859
all on local infrastructure rather than the cloud.

58
00:03:42,859 --> 00:03:47,440
This prototype was built by a focused team to prove the concept quickly.

59
00:03:47,440 --> 00:03:49,907
If AZ wants to take it further,

60
00:03:49,907 --> 00:03:54,840
Xebia Netherlands can bring additional AI and engineering capacity to scale the next phase.

61
00:03:55,140 --> 00:04:00,540
Xebia Netherlands — scalable AI & engineering capacity for a next phase

